{"kind":"assert","text":"SB-1 before-source comparison under26826de9, main694a8c7f83deedb4e8d34706c51203029726121a. No source change or executable validation. I recommend the single write guard plus a static bound witness; no scheduling-reader changes.\n\n1. A bound as a function, not a fixture.\n\nLet S be a valid natural sendsPerEntry \u003e=1 that bounds a scope's genesis. For a clean scope folded under that bound, define digits(n) as decimal digits and\nH(S)=2+sum(n=1..S-1,digits(n))+max(0,S-2).\nThen canonical scope metadata is at most B(S)=905+H(S) bytes. This is a conservative structural upper bound, not a claim that every simultaneous maximum is reachable under every definition.\n\nThe905 bytes exclude held's value: outer eight-key punctuation71; at151; creator151; genesis375 (hash73,n16,source FactRef262); head105 (hash73,seq16); kind13; status13; time26. ScopeState has exactly those eight fields (derive/state.ts:20-29, fold.ts:323). Scope IDs are55 characters (bytes/domains.ts:128-132), digests71 (hash.ts:33-34), safe nonnegative integer text at most16 digits (records.ts:48), longest kind destination is11 characters (ids.ts:14), and canonical timestamps at most24 (ids.ts:60-74; derive/time.ts:24-29 rejects invalid clocks). These encodings are ASCII, so byte length equals character length.\n\nThe held array is initially the nonzero sends of a provisional genesis (fold.ts:97). A child result owns ordinal0, body sends begin at1 (genesis.ts:174-175,229,233); sends.ts:192 assigns contiguous ordinals. validate/handlers.ts:129-136 and233 count declared sends, attention and the platform result INSIDE S. Thus held is a subset of1..S-1. Later entries only preserve it or clear it on activate (fold.ts:166-168), never add to it. Other Bounds fields cannot enlarge the fixed metadata encoding; entryBytes can only further restrict realizable genesis sends, so the sufficient function needs no entryBytes term. The retained entry count cannot bound lifetime seq after floors, so the calculation uses the16-digit safe-integer width.\n\nFor default S104, digits1..103 total201, held \u003c=305 bytes, B=1210, leaving2886 bytes below PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES4096. S\u003c=825 gives B\u003c=4094; S826 gives a conservative4098, which means this proof no longer suffices, not that every such definition writes an oversized row. For invalid/nonfinite S or untrusted existing metadata this clean-fold function establishes no bound. If wiring shrinks after birth, current S alone is insufficient: fold preserves held from the earlier bound. One must use the genesis bound/historical maximum or inspect the actual row.\n\n2. Wiring versus write, and refusal semantics.\n\nA wiring check B(S)\u003c=PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES can reject unsafe configuration before object construction. It is sufficient for a clean scope that always had those bounds, but rejects the entire object even if its actual definition/held row is small. It does not cover a row born under larger bounds or arbitrary typed setScope input.\n\nAn exact byte check in SqliteStore.setScope at scope/sqlite.ts:586-588 covers every supplied row independent of configurable bounds/history. Canonicalize once, compare UTF8 length to the imported PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES, and refuse before reading old scope/issuing the INSERT or held-outbox updates. An in-bound write follows today's code. An over-bound write must throw an internal bound error that unwinds the transaction and yields existing unavailable behavior, with no new public reason/API. It must never truncate held or partly commit genesis/journal/state.\n\nImportant transaction detail: the owning #seal already appended the entry before applyEntry/setScope (turn.ts:524,533). Do NOT throw the existing Unfit from this late guard: #judged catches Unfit at459-460 INSIDE the transaction and returns a committed unfit answer. Handle a dedicated metadata-bound error OUTSIDE the transaction at #commit:420-424, analogously to PinUnavailable, so waiting input gets {end:unavailable} after rollback. The timed outer handler at386-387 must also treat it as unfit so the turn ends unavailable with transition still due (367-369). Unhandled generic errors would rollback too but turn into RPC errors; I do not recommend that semantics. Any separate setScope user gets the typed error before its SQL mutation.\n\n3. Hosted rooms today, from source and recorded deployment, without live reads.\n\nRecorded cutover END53d5a148 says demo/grow/lending run5b342ee9c484c0c653dc1a363b852be83ef1d77b, with new registers/rooms and old rooms retired. That head and main694 have identical bounds, genesis/send numbering/validation and hosted wiring files. DeployedScope at scope/worker.ts:801-814 returns no bounds override; apps/room/worker.ts reexports it; Grow worker.grow.ts:19-29 adds archive wiring but no bounds. Object.ts:205 therefore chooses PROPOSED_BOUNDS, S104/E262144. The normal production fold in those hosted rooms cannot produce a row above1210 bytes. This is a source/recorded-deployment proof, not a live census or an assertion about manual corruption. artroom-scope/dap-connect were outside that cutover; I make no inventory claim about their retained rows from this record alone. No supported hosted configuration path supplies a higher S.\n\nRecommendation: enforce the actual writer bound and tie the static clean-default proof to PREPARATION_SCOPE_BYTES. This preserves all in-bound alarm recovery, including in-bound rows whose later preparation fails; it avoids a list of scheduling-read exceptions. It newly refuses a custom/raw write that would create peer-unreadable scope metadata. That changes that write's commit outcome, so the refusal must be explicit and atomic as above; I do not claim every unrelated old API was already unusable.\n\nMeaningful witnesses once source is authorized after AE-1: a static proof helper/test ties the exact field grammar, contiguous held bound and PROPOSED_BOUNDS to the shared constant; actual writer boundary accepts4096 and refuses4097 with no SQL/outbox mutation; owning-turn witness forces the guard after append/fold and proves transaction rollback, unchanged journal/head/outbox, no sealed callback and unavailable result. Preserve the existing real-object in-bound archive alarm recovery witness. The boundary tests protect the byte check; the outer transaction witness protects against the tempting wrong Unfit catch. No reader edits, native turn behavior changes for in-bound rows, or live reads are proposed. Planner ruling remains required before code; SB-1 still follows AE-1 and holds nothing.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:26826de9db9221ed91bfce12c3759154d709e3c9, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:836283176f960b8c886eeab41ee36ca4b83316e3 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5750e6e82650d29470d64f83b0dc3a50115b466"}}